# Candidate A: opt-in worker analytics

## Usage
```ts
// Existing entity-details and other tests: no changes.
import { test, expect } from "./backend";
test("entity details", async ({ page }) => { /* existing assertions */ });

// search-tracking.spec.ts: add to the existing file-level test.use.
test.use({ analytics: true, userAgent: existingChromeUserAgent });
test("each executed search emits one event", async ({ page, backend }) => {
  // Existing fake-host route decodes and records events before page.goto.
});
```

## Problem
The worker fixture unconditionally supplies fake analytics credentials, so every
application initializes PostHog and produces cross-origin requests. Fulfilling
those requests still exposes them to same-origin traffic assertions. The runtime
public environment and client hook already support disabling analytics with an
empty token or host; the fixture should express which workers need analytics.

## Shape
```ts
type WorkerFixtures = {
  analytics: boolean;
  application: { url: string; backend: Backend };
};
export const test = base.extend<{ backend: Backend }, WorkerFixtures>({
  analytics: [false, { option: true, scope: "worker" }],
  application: [async ({ analytics }, use, workerInfo) => {
    // Existing server lifecycle; child environment overrides process.env:
    // PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN: analytics ? "phc_test_search_tracking" : ""
    // PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST: analytics ? "https://posthog.test" : ""
    throw new Error("not implemented");
  }, { scope: "worker" }],
  // Keep existing page fixture's fake-host fulfillment before yielding page.
  // Keep baseURL and backend fixtures unchanged.
});
```
`analytics` is worker scoped because application environment is immutable after
spawn. Playwright separates incompatible worker fixture options; callers cannot
accidentally toggle an already-running application. Always override both inherited
environment values, including the disabled case, to avoid real credentials.
The existing page interceptor remains unconditional and serves as a fallback;
the tracking test's later route takes precedence and captures payloads. There is
one public boolean because the domain has two supported test states, with no
user-supplied host/token combinations. This hides process setup behind test.use
and follows boundary-discipline and separate-before-serializing-shared-state.

## Tradeoffs accepted
- Accept an extra worker for analytics tests in exchange for deterministic isolation.
- Accept a harmless default fake-host route in all tests to retain a safe fallback.

## Alternatives considered
A separately exported analyticsTest fixture hides opt-in configuration but adds a
second entry point and fixture composition for a single boolean distinction.
Globally enabling analytics and relaxing the traffic assertion weakens the
existing test's same-origin contract and fails to isolate the cause.

## Synthesis decision
Pending orchestrator comparison.

## Implementation reconciliation
No implementation or repository modifications performed.

## Risks and next step
Worker options must be selected at file scope, not inside test bodies or nested
describe groups. Add the option, run tracking and entity-details together, then
the complete browser suite. Confirm disabled workers generate no analytics
requests and enabled workers capture six expected search events without ingestion.
## Candidate B: Separate analytics fixture export

## Usage (caller's view)
```ts
// entity-details.spec.ts and all ordinary browser specs remain unchanged.
import { expect, test } from "./backend";
test("browser traffic stays inside Kit", async ({ page }) => { /* existing */ });

// search-tracking.spec.ts changes only its fixture import.
import { expect, analyticsTest as test } from "./backend";
test("each executed search emits one event", async ({ backend, page }) => {
  // Existing capture route and analytics assertions remain here.
});
```

## Problem
`backend.ts` currently injects a fake token and external host into every worker's
application process. Its default page route fulfills those external requests but
the entity-details request listener still sees them, violating same-origin checks.
Ordinary app workers must disable analytics, including inherited production env.

## Shape
Keep both exports in backend.ts; avoid a new module or public factory.
```ts
type Application = { url: string; backend: Backend };
type AnalyticsMode = "disabled" | "capture";

// Private factory: creates a distinct worker fixture definition for each export.
function createApplicationTest(mode: AnalyticsMode) {
  // TODO: existing base.extend<{ backend: Backend }, { application: Application }>.
  // Existing application lifecycle, backend, and baseURL stay together here.
  // Spawn env spreads process.env FIRST, then overrides:
  // disabled => PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN: "", PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST: ""
  // capture => fake token and https://posthog.test.
  // No general page override here.
  throw new Error("not implemented");
}
export const test = createApplicationTest("disabled");
export const analyticsTest = createApplicationTest("capture").extend({
  page: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await page.route("https://posthog.test/**", route => route.fulfill({ json: {} }));
    await use(page);
  },
});
```
Distinct application fixture definitions prevent worker reuse across modes.
Current workerIndex-based ports remain sufficient for separate Playwright workers.
Only analyticsTest installs the fake-host safety route; the spec's later route
continues capturing payloads using Playwright's latest-handler-first semantics.
One module owns startup, teardown, mode env, and interception defaults; callers
select a complete fixture using one import, per boundary-discipline.

## Tradeoffs accepted
- A second named export makes selection explicit; no new test.use option exists.
- A tiny private factory avoids duplicating the process lifecycle, at the cost of
  indenting the existing fixture definition and starting distinct mode workers.

## Alternatives considered
A worker-scoped option on the shared fixture needs fewer exports, but exposes mode
configuration to every spec and relies on each analytics caller setting it.
A separate module would need an exported factory or duplicated lifecycle; neither
improves the isolation provided by distinct fixture exports here.

## Synthesis decision and implementation reconciliation
Pending parent comparison; no repository implementation performed.

## Verification and next implementation step
Implement the private factory and switch the tracking import; run tracking and
the entity-details same-origin test together, then the existing browser suite.
Inspect ordinary-worker env to ensure inherited real credentials are overridden.
# Analytics fixture design

## Caller usage

Keep the shared backend fixture analytics-free. Opt in only where a test needs the browser SDK:

```ts
// tests/search-tracking.spec.ts
import { expect, test } from "./backend";

test.use({ posthog: true });
```

The entity-details same-origin traffic test imports the same `test` and does not opt in. It receives a fresh app worker without PostHog environment values, so browser requests continue to exercise the app's same-origin path.

## Fixture shape

```ts
type Fixtures = { backend: Backend };
type WorkerFixtures = {
  posthog: boolean; // Worker startup configuration; default false.
  application: { url: string; backend: Backend };
};

export const test = base.extend<Fixtures & { posthog: boolean }, WorkerFixtures>({
  posthog: [false, { option: true, scope: "worker" }],
  application: [async ({ posthog }, use, workerInfo) => {
    const env = {
      ...process.env,
      ...(posthog
        ? {
            PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN: "phc_test_search_tracking",
            PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST: "https://posthog.test",
          }
        : {}),
      PORT: String(4300 + workerInfo.workerIndex),
      // HOST and EXPOSED_API_ORIGIN remain as today.
    };
    // Spawn, readiness polling, use, and cleanup remain as today.
  }, { scope: "worker" }],
  // Existing page, baseURL, and backend fixtures remain unchanged.
});
```

`posthog` is a Playwright worker option, so its value is part of worker identity. Tests with `posthog: true` get an app process started with the fake analytics config; tests with the default `false` get an app process without it. Each worker has its own process and backend as it does now.

## Why this shape

This makes app startup configuration explicit at the test call site and keeps the worker fixture deterministic. The option fixture is the smallest addition: one typed boolean, one conditional environment fragment, and one opt-in in the tracking spec. A separate fixture module would split shared application setup or require another test export while adding little isolation. Mutating `process.env` is weaker: it changes runner-wide state and can race with parallel worker startup, even if code tries to restore the old values. Passing the option into the worker and constructing that child's environment avoids both problems.

